PATCH 482: four c0e5-dispatched per-object UPDATE methods took the object from the WRONG parameter and so
wrote every field through a stale stack slot.

The c0e5 walk (patch 243) dispatches `call [bx-0x1bac]` with the object's DGROUP near offset live in DI and
passes it as ARG0, so the object is param_1 -- confirmed at runtime for the whole table (7c1d gets 0xc05c,
bc46 gets 0xa090/0xa0c7/... , all valid slots) and already assumed by every other patched method.  Three
patches picked a later varnode:
  9afc + bc0c (patch 461) took param_2, and bc46 (patch 415) took param_3 -- at runtime those hold their
  own code address (0xbc0c) or a host stack address (0xffffcc68).  bc0c therefore OR-ed 0x40/0x44 into
  DGROUP:0xbc22/0xbc23 every frame a wreck existed: the first native<->wasm divergence of the AZER1
  self-play, found by diffing a per-tick 32-block DGROUP fingerprint between the targets (t=290, block
  0xbc00..0xbdff) and then watchpointing the word.
  9aa1 (patch 414) took param_2 for its age counter, so objects aged a random DGROUP word instead of
  their own [di+0x19].
Also corrected the widths these three used: [di+0x19]/[di+0x1f]/[di+0x21] are WORDs in the asm (the 32-bit
`int` accesses clobbered the neighbouring field), and 9aa1's milestone list / bc0c's smoke gate now read
the WORD the asm reads.
